kens candidate's case, and be consistent with vote.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. We can either agree with Candidate C, or propose another candidate. Let's examine remaining under-discussed aspects: reliability of A, stress resilience of D, communication of A and B, adaptability of C, etc. We could raise concerns about Candidate C's unorganized and poor criticism handling, which may be serious for safety-critical role. Perhaps we could argue that Candidate A's reliability (100% reliable) and quick correct decisions, plus positive crew atmosphere, outweigh his communication difficulty, and that his egocentric trait may be mitigated. Or we could argue Candidate B's stress handling and conscientiousness, but communication tone issues.\n\nWe need to bring new evidence: we have from our notes: Candidate A can make correct decisions quickly; excellent attention skills; puts concern for others above everything. Also Candidate B handles stress very well; good at assessing weather; excellent computer skills; very conscientious. Candidate D responds to unexpected events adequately; can concentrate very well; solves problems extremely well; takes responsibility seriously.\n\nWe could ask a question to other agents for missing info, but we can also just argue with existing facts. The panel hasn't discussed reliability of A beyond 100% reliable (Agent 1 said 100% reliable). Actually Agent 1's note says \"100% reliable (reliability)\". So reliability is strong for A. Stress resilience for D not discussed. Could ask about D's stress resilience. But we can also argue that Candidate A's reliability and decision speed are crucial for long-distance flights, and his positive crew atmosphere suggests good cooperation despite egocentric trait. Meanwhile Candidate C's unorganized and poor feedback handling could be a risk.\n\nThus we could shift recommendation to Candidate A. Or we could keep with C but highlight need to address adaptability. The instruction: we must make a focused argumentative contribution, not just ask. We can change vote if we have reason.\n\nGiven the panel hasn't fully covered reliability and stress for others, we could argue that Candidate A's reliability and decision quality are paramount, and his egocentric trait may be less critical than C's unorganized nature. So we could propose Candidate A.\n\nWe need to reference existing discussion: Agent 1's recommendation for C, Agent 2's support. We can say \"I see the strengths of C, but I'm concerned about his unorganized nature and poor handling of criticism, which could affect safety. In contrast, Candidate A is 100% reliable, makes quick correct decisions, has excellent attention, and creates a positive crew atmosphere, which may outweigh his communication difficulty.\"\n\nThus we vote for Candidate A.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: \"100% reliable\", \"can make correct decisions quickly\", \"excellent attention skills\", \"creates positive atmosphere with crew\", \"egocentric\", \"difficulty communicating ideas\". Also note \"puts concern for others above everything\". Use these to argue.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate A."}
